WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.650
A well-crafted plot twist can take a movie
from good to great—in fact, some movies

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.120
are regarded as classics specifically because
of twists that completely recontextualize

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:10.680
the story.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.839
But sending the narrative in an unexpected
direction sometimes just ends up making a

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.340
film worse.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.720
As great as it feels to be surprised by an
excellent plot twist, these movies would have

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:20.660
been better off without them.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.960
As always, beware the spoilers ahead.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:24.880
Remember Me

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:30.280
There's nothing wrong with a simple romance
movie, and as far as that element goes, Remember

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.440
Me isn't too bad.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.250
Its leads, Robert Pattinson and Emilie de
Ravin, are solid enough

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.220
in their roles and boast some nice chemistry.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.380
And while it may veer into cliché, the film
still certainly has its charms.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.500
But all of that goes completely and inexplicably
down the drain in the movie's final moments.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.440
The film has become infamous for its final
act, which reveals that the entire story has

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.540
been leading up to Robert Pattinson's character
being in the World Trade Center on September

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.040
11th.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.880
It's a subject matter that Remember Me bungles
badly, taking a horrific tragedy and using

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.900
it as cheap emotional manipulation.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.180
"I mean, why aren't you RIVETED?"

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.240
Aside from being deeply insensitive, it's
also a case of blown potential, as the romantic

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.120
drama is just fine without resorting to the
careless and contrived finale.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:17.580
The Village

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.100
The biggest problem with The Village is that
its twist turns it into a much worse movie.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.220
Writer-director M. Night Shyamalan's last-act
plot twists helped launch his career, but

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.560
they ended up becoming notorious to the point
of parody after this one.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.700
The horror film appears to center on a small
19th century village, which has been surrounded

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.280
by mysterious monsters, but it's actually
revealed that the creatures don't exist.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.800
They're just the town elders in costume, trying
to scare people into staying put in their

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:46.660
safe community.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.380
That's all well and good enough until the
second twist arrives to reveal the film actually

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.970
takes place in the modern day, and the village
elders created these monsters to keep their

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.680
people protected from the woes of the outside
world.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.660
With just the first twist in place, the movie
might work as solid social commentary, but

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.380
the second rug pull makes the movie needlessly
convoluted.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.140
Spectre

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.700
Given the film's title, it's not surprising
that Spectre centers on the villainous organization

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:19.950
headed up by Ernst Blofeld, one of 007's greatest
villains, or that Christoph Waltz was brought

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:24.060
in to portray the criminal mastermind, despite
some slack attempts at secrecy.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.900
"So, thank you, cuckoo."

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.240
But instead of simply rebooting the iconic
villain, Waltz's character is also eventually

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.300
revealed to be Bond's adoptive brother, making
a surprise return after faking his own death.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:43.780
The twist is completely superfluous: the original
Blofeld is a force of pure evil, and he doesn't

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.440
need to be tied to Bond in any way to be effective.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.260
The revelation that Bond has a secret brother
who ended up orchestrating the events in the

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:56.880
previous Daniel Craig-led Bond films strains
all credibility for his run in the franchise.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.200
"It was me, James.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.860
The author of all your pain."

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.040
Put simply, it's a twist that twists too hard.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:06.620
Savages

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.020
Oliver Stone's film adaptation of Savages
initially seems to end like the book— with

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.700
the demise of basically every character providing
a sort of closure.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.980
It also fits in well with the way the storytellers
want viewers to see the three main characters—they're

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.360
too wild for this world, and it only makes
sense for them to go out in a blaze of glory.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:31.300
Unfortunately, Stone tacks on a twist wherein
the ending is revealed to be an elaborate

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.700
fantasy playing out in the head of one character.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.320
Instead, the bad guys get arrested and the
good guys get out clean, riding off into the

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:38.820
sunset.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.420
This disjointed happy ending isn't just a
cheat—it feels like a painfully safe move.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.020
High Tension

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.180
When crafting a good plot twist, it's incredibly
important to consider whether it upends the

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.560
plausibility of the story.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.280
Unfortunately for Alexandre Aja's High Tension,
its Hail Mary of a twist ending—in which

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:01.540
the killer is revealed to be a dissociative
identity of the protagonist—sends plausibility

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.320
down the drain.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.860
The film, up until that point, is a pretty
straightforward slasher that stands out for

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:07.900
its artful gore.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.740
So why the needless twist?

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:14.620
Shots filmed from the villain's point of view
make little sense after the twist, and the

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:18.000
film's opening is even more confusing when
it's watched while knowing who the killer

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:18.840
turns out to be.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.460
Secret Window

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.280
Speaking of misuse of dissociative identity
disorder, Secret Window also stumbles in its

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.480
presentation of the illness.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.660
It should be a slam dunk of a movie.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.340
Based on a Stephen King short story, it's
about a writer who retreats to a cabin in

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.720
the woods to craft his next book, only to
be confronted by a deranged stranger named

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.500
Shooter who claims he has plagiarized one
of his works.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.000
"You stole my story."

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.980
Tensions between the two escalate and eventually
turn to violence.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.740
The twist is that Shooter doesn't exist and
is instead a result of our lead's mental unwellness,

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.620
which—conveniently—has never manifested
in the movie until this moment.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.340
Sometimes this twist works, like with Fight
Club and Primal Fear.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.860
But in Secret Window, it comes off as uninspired,
which is ironic considering how much we hear

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.260
about stories needing to stick the landing
throughout.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.100
"The only thing that matters is the ending."

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:14.940
Scream 3

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.360
The first film in the Scream franchise features
a twist that is revelatory for the horror

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.000
genre: there are two culprits behind the film's
slaughter spree, not just one.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.060
"Surprise Sidney."

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:33.300
The twist is so good that it's repeated in
the sequel, Scream 2, and still mostly works.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:37.920
For the third film, though, the filmmakers
go with a new twist, it ends up being just

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:38.600
plain dumb.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.280
Part of the reason the first two films in
the franchise enjoy such reverence is that

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:48.160
they function as critiques of the genre—they're
self-aware, and skewer the typical tropes

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.560
that had started to bore audiences.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:55.200
The problem with Scream 3's needlessly complicated
reveal is that it resembles something from

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.720
the films the franchise itself satirizes.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:03.500
The revelation that the baddie in this film
is Scream protagonist Sidney's secret half-brother—and

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:08.720
that he has effectively orchestrated the events
of the entire series—is completely unbelievable.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:14.000
It's a jump-the-shark moment in a franchise
that scoffs at cliche and closes out the original

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.140
series with a whimper instead of, well, a
scream.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:21.960
But, hey, credit to Stu for trying to tie
everything together and save the story…

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:22.580
again.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:27.680
"If you find yourself dealing with a backstory
and a preponderance of exposition, then the

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.560
sequel rules do not apply."

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.140
Thanks for watching!

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.500
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:35.880
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

